import{_ as i,o as t,c as a,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.CqpfiAoK.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Nordic Welfare Civilization","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/32-北欧福利型文明.md","filePath":"en/32-北欧福利型文明.md","lastUpdated":1789183869000}'),n={name:"en/32-北欧福利型文明.md"};function r(l,e,s,c,h,u){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="nordic-welfare-civilization" tabindex="-1">Nordic Welfare Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#nordic-welfare-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Nordic Welfare Civilization&quot;">​</a></h1><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document No.</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Module</td><td>Civilization Typologies and Social Systems</td></tr><tr><td>Keywords</td><td>Nordic Welfare, High Taxation, High Equality, Universal Protection, Happiness Index</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="i-origins-and-core-concepts-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" tabindex="-1">I. Origins and Core Concepts of the Nordic Welfare Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-origins-and-core-concepts-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I. Origins and Core Concepts of the Nordic Welfare Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Nordic Welfare Civilization is the most egalitarian of the five symbiotic civilization typologies, with origins traceable to the social models of Nordic countries in the real world (Sweden, Norway, Denmark, Finland, Iceland). Through long-term development, Nordic countries formed the &quot;Nordic model&quot; centered on high taxation, high welfare, and high equality, achieving a good balance between economic development and social equity, consistently ranking at the top of global happiness index lists. After the establishment of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization, this model combined with symbiotic concepts and was upgraded to create the unique civilizational form of the &quot;Nordic Welfare Civilization.&quot;</p><p>The core concept of the Nordic Welfare Civilization can be summarized in nine characters: &quot;high welfare, high equality, high happiness.&quot; Specifically:</p><ol><li><strong>High welfare</strong>: the state provides every citizen with comprehensive welfare protection &quot;from cradle to grave,&quot; including education, medical care, housing, elderly care, unemployment, and all other aspects, ensuring every citizen can live a dignified life</li><li><strong>High equality</strong>: through high taxation and high transfer payments, greatly narrowing the wealth gap, achieving relatively balanced distribution of social wealth. In the Nordic Welfare Civilization, the realm gap and resource gap among cultivators are controlled within a reasonable range</li><li><strong>High happiness</strong>: high welfare and high equality bring high happiness. Citizens of the Nordic Welfare Civilization generally have high life satisfaction, mental health levels, and social trust</li></ol><p>These concepts of the Nordic Welfare Civilization are highly compatible with the &quot;A Path for Everyone&quot; core concept of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization. High welfare guarantees every citizen&#39;s basic right to evolve, high equality embodies the spirit of &quot;common progress of the Myriad Races,&quot; and high happiness is one of the ultimate goals pursued by the symbiotic civilization.</p><h2 id="ii-the-social-structure-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" tabindex="-1">II. The Social Structure of the Nordic Welfare Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#ii-the-social-structure-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;II. The Social Structure of the Nordic Welfare Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The social structure of the Nordic Welfare Civilization takes &quot;high-welfare state + flat society + strong civil society&quot; as its basic features:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Structural Element</th><th>Feature Description</th><th>Core Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>High-Welfare State</td><td>The government assumes comprehensive welfare protection responsibility, with taxes accounting for a relatively high proportion of GDP</td><td>Providing universal public services and social protection</td></tr><tr><td>Flat Society</td><td>Small social class gaps, weak hierarchical concepts, relatively equal relations among people</td><td>Reducing social conflicts, enhancing social cohesion</td></tr><tr><td>Strong Civil Society</td><td>Civil society organizations such as NGOs, trade unions, and cooperatives are well developed</td><td>Supplementing government services, protecting citizens&#39; rights, promoting social participation</td></tr><tr><td>High-Trust Culture</td><td>High mutual trust between citizens and between citizens and the government</td><td>Reducing social transaction costs, improving social operational efficiency</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="_2-1-high-welfare-state" tabindex="-1">2.1 High-Welfare State <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-1-high-welfare-state" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.1 High-Welfare State&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The government of the Nordic Welfare Civilization is a typical &quot;high-welfare state.&quot; By levying a relatively high proportion of taxes (including energy tax, resource tax, income tax, etc.), it establishes a welfare protection system covering the entire population. Welfare expenditures are mainly used for:</p><ul><li><strong>Education welfare</strong>: from initiation education to higher cultivation education, all provided free of charge</li><li><strong>Medical welfare</strong>: basic medical care and cultivation injury treatment all free</li><li><strong>Housing welfare</strong>: providing decent housing protection for every citizen</li><li><strong>Elderly care welfare</strong>: providing sufficient pensions and elderly care services for retired cultivators</li><li><strong>Unemployment welfare</strong>: providing basic livelihood protection for citizens temporarily unable to cultivate or work</li></ul><p>The core concept of the high-welfare state is &quot;universality&quot; — welfare is not relief targeted at the poor but a universal right enjoyed by all citizens. Every citizen, whether rich or poor, regardless of cultivation realm, has the right to enjoy equal welfare protection.</p><h3 id="_2-2-flat-society" tabindex="-1">2.2 Flat Society <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-2-flat-society" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.2 Flat Society&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The social structure of the Nordic Welfare Civilization is very flat, with small class gaps and weak hierarchical concepts. In the cultivation field, this is reflected in:</p><ul><li>The resource gap between high-level and junior cultivators is controlled within a reasonable range</li><li>High-level cultivators have no privileges above junior cultivators</li><li>Communication among cultivators is equal and respectful, not condescending</li><li>Social status depends more on personal character and contribution than on the height of cultivation realm</li></ul><p>This flat social structure greatly reduces social conflicts and enhances social cohesion. Cultivators need not cultivate desperately to pursue higher social status, and can develop more calmly according to their own pace and interests.</p><h3 id="_2-3-strong-civil-society" tabindex="-1">2.3 Strong Civil Society <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-3-strong-civil-society" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2.3 Strong Civil Society&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Nordic Welfare Civilization has a developed civil society, with NGOs, trade unions, cooperatives, and other civil society organizations playing important roles in social life:</p><ul><li><strong>Trade union organizations</strong>: representing cultivators in consultation with management institutions, protecting cultivators&#39; legitimate rights and interests</li><li><strong>Cooperative organizations</strong>: cooperative economic organizations voluntarily formed by cultivators, jointly operating cultivation resources and sharing income</li><li><strong>Interest clubs</strong>: club organizations based on common interests and cultivation directions, promoting exchange and cooperation among cultivators</li><li><strong>Public welfare organizations</strong>: non-profit organizations engaged in public welfare undertakings and helping vulnerable groups</li></ul><p>The strong civil society supplements the shortcomings of government services, providing citizens with more participation channels and choice space, and also enhancing society&#39;s self-regulating capacity.</p><h2 id="iii-the-cultivation-model-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" tabindex="-1">III. The Cultivation Model of the Nordic Welfare Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#iii-the-cultivation-model-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;III. The Cultivation Model of the Nordic Welfare Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The cultivation model of the Nordic Welfare Civilization takes &quot;universal, personalized, low-pressure&quot; as its core features:</p><h3 id="_3-1-universal-cultivation-protection" tabindex="-1">3.1 Universal Cultivation Protection <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-universal-cultivation-protection" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1 Universal Cultivation Protection&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Nordic Welfare Civilization provides every citizen with sufficient basic cultivation resources, including:</p><ul><li>Free basic cultivation methods and textbooks</li><li>Sufficient basic energy allocations</li><li>Free basic cultivation guidance</li><li>Free access to cultivation facilities</li></ul><p>This universal protection ensures every citizen can begin cultivation without economic pressure, truly realizing &quot;A Path for Everyone.&quot;</p><h3 id="_3-2-personalized-cultivation-support" tabindex="-1">3.2 Personalized Cultivation Support <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-2-personalized-cultivation-support" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.2 Personalized Cultivation Support&quot;">​</a></h3><p>On the basis of universal protection, the Nordic Welfare Civilization places great importance on personalized cultivation support. Every cultivator can obtain professional cultivation assessment and personalized cultivation advice. Cultivation guidance teachers will tailor cultivation plans according to each cultivator&#39;s characteristics, interests, and goals.</p><p>The Nordic Welfare Civilization does not encourage the &quot;thousands of troops crossing a single-plank bridge&quot; style of standardized cultivation, but respects each cultivator&#39;s personality differences and encourages diversified development directions. Cultivators can choose cultivation systems according to their interests, without blindly pursuing the &quot;strongest&quot; system.</p><h3 id="_3-3-low-pressure-cultivation-environment" tabindex="-1">3.3 Low-Pressure Cultivation Environment <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-3-low-pressure-cultivation-environment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.3 Low-Pressure Cultivation Environment&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Nordic Welfare Civilization deliberately creates a low-pressure cultivation environment:</p><ul><li>No intense elimination mechanisms; cultivators will not be abandoned for slow progress</li><li>Cultivation assessments focus on encouragement rather than screening</li><li>Cultivators have ample rest and entertainment time, not encouraged to &quot;seclude for bitter cultivation&quot;</li><li>Society does not use cultivation realm as the only standard for evaluating personal value</li></ul><p>This low-pressure cultivation environment enables cultivators to maintain a good mentality and enjoy the process of cultivation, rather than treating cultivation as a painful competition.</p><h3 id="_3-4-work-cultivation-balance" tabindex="-1">3.4 Work-Cultivation Balance <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-4-work-cultivation-balance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.4 Work-Cultivation Balance&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Nordic Welfare Civilization emphasizes the balance between work and cultivation. Cultivators need not invest all their time in cultivation, but can simultaneously engage in work, family, hobbies, and other activities. The civilization encourages &quot;fully developed people&quot; rather than &quot;machines that only cultivate.&quot;</p><h2 id="iv-the-output-linked-contract-mechanism-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" tabindex="-1">IV. The Output-Linked Contract Mechanism of the Nordic Welfare Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#iv-the-output-linked-contract-mechanism-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IV. The Output-Linked Contract Mechanism of the Nordic Welfare Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The output-linked contract mechanism of the Nordic Welfare Civilization takes &quot;high-welfare contracting&quot; as its main form:</p><ol><li><strong>High basic allocation</strong>: the allocation standard of basic guarantee fields is higher, ensuring every citizen can obtain sufficient basic cultivation resources</li><li><strong>High surplus to public</strong>: a higher proportion of the surplus energy and resources produced by cultivators is allocated to the public welfare pool, supporting universal welfare</li><li><strong>Gentle incentive</strong>: the incentive mechanism of advancement contract fields is relatively gentle; high contributors can obtain more resources, but the gap will not be too large</li><li><strong>Welfare orientation</strong>: the use of public resources prioritizes welfare protection, ensuring every citizen&#39;s basic needs are met</li></ol><p>The advantage of this &quot;high-welfare contracting&quot; mechanism is the realization of high social equality and citizen happiness, but it also needs to pay attention to avoiding the problem of &quot;raising the lazy&quot; — through reasonable incentive design, encouraging citizens to actively contribute while enjoying welfare.</p><h2 id="v-advantages-and-challenges-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" tabindex="-1">V. Advantages and Challenges of the Nordic Welfare Civilization <a class="header-anchor" href="#v-advantages-and-challenges-of-the-nordic-welfare-civilization" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;V. Advantages and Challenges of the Nordic Welfare Civilization&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_5-1-typical-advantages" tabindex="-1">5.1 Typical Advantages <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-1-typical-advantages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.1 Typical Advantages&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Strong happiness</strong>: high welfare and high equality bring extremely high citizen happiness and life satisfaction</li><li><strong>High equality</strong>: small wealth and class gaps, few social conflicts</li><li><strong>Social stability</strong>: high welfare guarantees long-term social stability and harmony</li><li><strong>High trust</strong>: high trust between citizens and between citizens and the government improves social operational efficiency</li></ul><h3 id="_5-2-facing-challenges" tabindex="-1">5.2 Facing Challenges <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-2-facing-challenges" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5.2 Facing Challenges&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>Weak innovation drive</strong>: high welfare and low pressure may weaken some people&#39;s enterprising spirit and innovation drive</li><li><strong>Heavier tax burden</strong>: high welfare requires high taxation support, which may affect the enthusiasm of high contributors</li><li><strong>Relatively low efficiency</strong>: overemphasis on equality may sacrifice efficiency to a certain extent</li><li><strong>Population structure pressure</strong>: if population aging intensifies, the sustainability of the welfare system will face challenges</li></ul><p>In response to these challenges, the Nordic Welfare Civilization is actively absorbing the advantages of other typologies, such as introducing the East Asian Intensive Type&#39;s efficiency mechanism and the North American Pluralistic Type&#39;s innovation incentives, enhancing development vitality while maintaining welfare characteristics.</p><h2 id="conclusion" tabindex="-1">Conclusion <a class="header-anchor" href="#conclusion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Conclusion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The Nordic Welfare Civilization is the most egalitarian civilization typology of the Anchor Symbiotic Civilization. With the core concept of &quot;high welfare, high equality, high happiness,&quot; it achieves high citizen happiness and high social equality through the social structure of the high-welfare state, flat society, and strong civil society, the universal personalized cultivation model, and the high-welfare-contracting output-link mechanism. While maintaining its own characteristics, the Nordic Welfare Civilization is also continuously exploring the best balance point between welfare and efficiency, protection and incentive.</p><h2 id="related-documents" tabindex="-1">Related Documents <a class="header-anchor" href="#related-documents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related Documents&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>30-五种共生文明分型总览 (Overview of the Five Symbiotic Civilization Typologies)</li><li>38-共生医疗体系 (Symbiotic Medical System)</li><li>39-住房与环境保障 (Housing and Environmental Protection)</li></ul>',51)])])}const p=i(n,[["render",r]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
